From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brad Barnett <127794626+bdbarnett@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 14:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] shared/tinyusb: stall a busy runtime IN endpoint with the USB
 interrupt held off on disconnect.

On an ESP32-S3 on its native USB, machine.USBDevice().active(False)
while a runtime interface (one defined from Python, such as
micropython-lib's usb-device-cdc) is sending hangs the board for good,
with no REPL on either port (micropython-pydevices#73). A soft reset with
the runtime device active takes the same path.

mp_usbd_disconnect() stalls every endpoint that has a transfer in flight.
Disabling a busy IN endpoint in TinyUSB 0.21's DWC2 driver (patch 0014)
polls DIEPINT for NAK-effective and then endpoint-disabled, and the
interrupt handler writes back every DIEPINT bit it reads. In slave mode
the TX-FIFO-empty interrupt keeps firing while a multi-packet transfer
runs, so one that lands between the hardware setting a flag and the poll
reading it clears the flag, and the poll spins forever. Patch 0025 fixed
the same race for the built-in CDC by stalling it with the USB interrupt
held off.

A busy runtime IN endpoint is now stalled the same way, in an atomic
section of its own. OUT endpoints are still stalled with the interrupt
running, because disabling one waits for the global OUT NAK, which in
slave mode only arrives when the interrupt pops it from the RX FIFO; the
OUT side's endpoint-disabled flag is one the handler never clears. The IN
half waits only on the hardware, so it finishes with the interrupt held
off.

The other ways to close the race live in the DWC2 driver: wait on the
endpoint's own enable bit (which the core clears and the handler never
writes) instead of the flags, or have the handler write back only the
bits it acts on. Both are the better fix upstream, but this port builds
TinyUSB from the IDF component manager, which this series can't patch,
and the atomic section is the mechanism patch 0025 already proved.

Proven on a Waveshare ESP32-S3-Touch-LCD-7 (ESP32_GENERIC_S3 LCD_7, with
usbif), driven from its UART console. Each trial soft-resets, builds a
runtime device, waits for Windows to configure it and open the port,
keeps the IN endpoint busy and calls active(False); the endpoint was
busy at every disconnect. All the failures were hangs:

  | runtime interface, how it is kept busy             | before | this patch |
  |----------------------------------------------------|--------|------------|
  | usb-device-cdc, one 16 KB write, disconnect 0-6 ms | 5/40   | 0/80       |
  | usb-device-cdc, writing for 150 ms, then 0-600 us  | 4/40   | 0/40       |
  | usb-device-mouse at a 1 ms interval, empty reports | 0/40   | 0/40       |

A HID report is a single packet, so its endpoint gives the interrupt no
window: that row passes either way. With the patch, patch 0025's own
trials on the built-in CDC (active(False) and the switch to a runtime
device, mid-print) were 0 in 40, and the friendly REPL, a 12 KB paste-mode
block, 4000 lines read back whole and in order, and mpftp (exec, a 78 KB
put with hash, run, rm) all worked over the CDC.

The ESP32-S2 and ESP32-P4 build TinyUSB 0.21 the same way, in slave mode
(CFG_TUD_DWC2_DMA_ENABLE defaults to 0), so they share the path.

Drop this patch when TinyUSB's DWC2 endpoint disable stops depending on
interrupt flags the handler can clear.

Based-on: micropython 0fd6c573e (tag v1.29.0)
---
 shared/tinyusb/mp_usbd_runtime.c | 16 +++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/shared/tinyusb/mp_usbd_runtime.c b/shared/tinyusb/mp_usbd_runtime.c
--- a/shared/tinyusb/mp_usbd_runtime.c
+++ b/shared/tinyusb/mp_usbd_runtime.c
@@ -476,7 +476,21 @@
         for (int epnum = 0; epnum < CFG_TUD_ENDPPOINT_MAX; epnum++) {
             for (int dir = 0; dir < 2; dir++) {
                 if (usbd->xfer_data[epnum][dir] != mp_const_none) {
-                    usbd_edpt_stall(RHPORT, tu_edpt_addr(epnum, dir));
+                    if (dir == TUSB_DIR_IN) {
+                        // A busy IN endpoint is stalled with the USB interrupt
+                        // held off, as the built-in CDC's is below: disabling
+                        // it polls for the NAK-effective and endpoint-disabled
+                        // flags, which the DWC2 interrupt (ESP32-S2/S3/P4)
+                        // clears if it runs in between, and the poll never
+                        // ends. An OUT endpoint is stalled with the interrupt
+                        // running: its disable waits on the global OUT NAK,
+                        // which only the interrupt's RX FIFO handling delivers.
+                        mp_uint_t atomic_state = MICROPY_BEGIN_ATOMIC_SECTION();
+                        usbd_edpt_stall(RHPORT, tu_edpt_addr(epnum, dir));
+                        MICROPY_END_ATOMIC_SECTION(atomic_state);
+                    } else {
+                        usbd_edpt_stall(RHPORT, tu_edpt_addr(epnum, dir));
+                    }
                     usbd->xfer_data[epnum][dir] = mp_const_none;
                 }
             }
